<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> was fought on 26 February 1266 near Benevento in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred, King of Sicily</a>. Manfred was killed and his army destroyed, giving Charles the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> power in southern Italy. The victory strengthened the Guelph cause across Italy, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> the Ghibellines lost control of the city soon afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento.jpg" alt="Battle of Benevento" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Villani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villani_Benevento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> had long been in conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> over their rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Hohenstaufen ruler of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which included Sicily and southern Italy, was Manfred, an illegitimate son of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. The rightful heir was Frederick's legitimate grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, aged 14. Manfred had been regent since 1254 and took the throne in 1258, using a false rumour of Conradin's death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> resolved to take the kingdom from him and in 1263 made a secret treaty promising the Sicilian throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>. After Urban's death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv">Pope Clement IV</a> continued the support.</p><p>Charles reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in May 1265 but was held up by the need to raise money. Manfred spent his time hunting, trusting his German knights and Saracen horsemen. He took the field only in January 1266, after Charles's main army had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Alarmed by the surrender of towns and castles and by desertions, Manfred sought a swift battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Charles tried to turn Manfred's position at Capua by crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, which wrecked his supply line. Manfred, informed of the move, waited across the River Calore, which could be crossed only by one bridge. Charles had about 12,000 men, nearly all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the French and Provençal crowns. Manfred's army was more mixed: Saracen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> in front, then 1,200 German mercenary knights and men-at-arms, then Italian mercenary cavalry and Saracen light horse, and finally the barons of the kingdom under his own command, whom he distrusted.</p><p>On the morning of 26 February 1266 Manfred sent his Saracens forward and Provençal sergeants overwhelmed them. His German knights then crossed the bridge to attack and at first pushed the Provençals back. Charles committed his second battle and 400 Italians. According to the chronicle of Andrew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, a French knight noticed that the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plate-armour">plate armour</a> left the armpits exposed when the arm was raised, and the shorter, sharper French blades did great damage in close fighting. The German corps was practically annihilated.</p><p>Manfred's second battle arrived late, after the long crossing of the bridge, and fell to Charles's third battle. Most of the nobles in his third corps deserted. Manfred, who had exchanged his royal surcoat with his friend Tebaldo Annibaldi, rode into the enemy and was killed. Many fugitives drowned in the flooded Calore, and only 600 of his 3,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> escaped death or capture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The destruction of Manfred's army ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The rest of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was conquered almost without resistance. Charles then awaited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, the last hope of the Hohenstaufen, and met him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tagliacozzo">Battle of Tagliacozzo</a> in 1268.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Benevento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Pope Clement IV invested Charles of Anjou, brother of Louis IX of France, with the kingdom; in February 1266 Charles defeated and killed King Manfred at the <b>Battle of Benevento</b> and in 1268 defeated the last Hohenstaufen heir, Conradin, at the Battle of Tagliacozzo, beheading him in Naples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Manfred, Frederick’s son, was killed at the <b>Battle of Benevento</b> in 1266 by Charles of Anjou, who then defeated Conradin at Tagliacozzo in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Montaperti in 1260 a Ghibelline army of Siena and Florentine exiles routed the Guelph forces of Florence, and the city was nearly destroyed; after the Angevin victory at the <b>Battle of Benevento</b> in 1266 the Guelphs returned for good.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li></ul></section>
